                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.00/11: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-12-16T15:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 16,
                                1922—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 16—10:26
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>497. My 480, December 6, 4 p.m. In spite of release accomplished or promised of
                    all foreigners, diplomatic body refused to dissolve international commission,
                    simply notifying Foreign Office that commission would not leave for Honan at
                    date arranged and adding that diplomatic body still viewed “with great anxiety
                    and concern the continued danger to their nationals residing in Honan” and
                    expressing hope “that there will be no relaxation in the efforts of the Chinese
                    authorities to return the Province to a state of peace and security.”</p>
                <p>Note in full published to-day.<anchor n="38" xml:id="d676-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
